      "examAnswer": "A set of processes is said to be in a state of deadlock when every process in the set is waiting for an event that can be caused only by another process in the same set, so that none of them can ever proceed. Deadlock can arise only if four conditions, known as the Coffman conditions, hold simultaneously. Mutual exclusion requires that at least one resource be held in a non-sharable mode; hold and wait requires that a process holding at least one resource be waiting to acquire additional resources held by others; no preemption requires that a resource be released only voluntarily by the process holding it; and circular wait requires the existence of a set of waiting processes P0 to Pn such that each is waiting for a resource held by the next and Pn is waiting for a resource held by P0. Since all four are necessary, the invalidation of any one of them is sufficient to prevent deadlock.",
