      "realLife": "Prevention is a hostel rule: 'collect your key, towel and bucket together at reception, or not at all'. Avoidance is a bank manager who checks your total sanctioned limit before releasing each instalment, so he never runs out of cash. Detection is a traffic policeman who arrives after the jam has formed and makes one car reverse.",
      "worked": "<strong>Banker's Algorithm — full worked example.</strong><br>Five processes P0-P4, three resource types A, B, C. Total instances: <strong>A = 10, B = 5, C = 7</strong>.<br><br><strong>Step 1 — The current state.</strong><br><code>Process | Allocation (A B C) | Max (A B C)</code><br><code>P0      |      0 1 0        |   7 5 3</code><br><code>P1      |      2 0 0        |   3 2 2</code><br><code>P2      |      3 0 2        |   9 0 2</code><br><code>P3      |      2 1 1        |   2 2 2</code><br><code>P4      |      0 0 2        |   4 3 3</code><br><br><strong>Step 2 — Find Available.</strong> Total allocated = A: 0+2+3+2+0 = 7, B: 1+0+0+1+0 = 2, C: 0+0+2+1+2 = 5.<br>Available = Total - Allocated = (10-7, 5-2, 7-5) = <strong>(3, 3, 2)</strong>.<br><br><strong>Step 3 — Compute Need = Max - Allocation.</strong><br><code>P0 Need = 7-0, 5-1, 3-0 = 7 4 3</code><br><code>P1 Need = 3-2, 2-0, 2-0 = 1 2 2</code><br><code>P2 Need = 9-3, 0-0, 2-2 = 6 0 0</code><br><code>P3 Need = 2-2, 2-1, 2-1 = 0 1 1</code><br><code>P4 Need = 4-0, 3-0, 3-2 = 4 3 1</code><br><br><strong>Step 4 — Run the safety algorithm.</strong> Work = Available = (3, 3, 2). All processes unfinished.<br><br><em>Pass 1:</em><br>P0? Need (7,4,3) vs Work (3,3,2) → 7 &gt; 3. <strong>Cannot run.</strong><br>P1? Need (1,2,2) vs Work (3,3,2) → 1≤3, 2≤3, 2≤2. <strong>YES.</strong> P1 runs and finishes.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Work = Work + Allocation(P1) = (3+2, 3+0, 2+0) = <strong>(5, 3, 2)</strong>. Sequence so far: P1.<br><br><em>Pass 2:</em><br>P2? Need (6,0,0) vs (5,3,2) → 6 &gt; 5. <strong>No.</strong><br>P3? Need (0,1,1) vs (5,3,2) → 0≤5, 1≤3, 1≤2. <strong>YES.</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Work = (5+2, 3+1, 2+1) = <strong>(7, 4, 3)</strong>. Sequence: P1, P3.<br><br><em>Pass 3:</em><br>P4? Need (4,3,1) vs (7,4,3) → 4≤7, 3≤4, 1≤3. <strong>YES.</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Work = (7+0, 4+0, 3+2) = <strong>(7, 4, 5)</strong>. Sequence: P1, P3, P4.<br><br><em>Pass 4:</em><br>P0? Need (7,4,3) vs (7,4,5) → 7≤7, 4≤4, 3≤5. <strong>YES.</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Work = (7+0, 4+1, 5+0) = <strong>(7, 5, 5)</strong>. Sequence: P1, P3, P4, P0.<br><br><em>Pass 5:</em><br>P2? Need (6,0,0) vs (7,5,5) → 6≤7. <strong>YES.</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Work = (7+3, 5+0, 5+2) = <strong>(10, 5, 7)</strong>. Sequence: P1, P3, P4, P0, P2.<br><br><strong>Answer: the state is SAFE, with safe sequence &lt;P1, P3, P4, P0, P2&gt;.</strong><br>Check: the final Work (10, 5, 7) equals the total resources — every process finished and returned everything. That check proves the sequence is genuinely safe.<br><br><strong>Step 5 — Now a request arrives: P1 requests (1, 0, 2).</strong><br>(a) Is Request ≤ Need(P1)? (1,0,2) ≤ (1,2,2)? Yes.<br>(b) Is Request ≤ Available? (1,0,2) ≤ (3,3,2)? Yes.<br>(c) Pretend to grant it:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Available = (3-1, 3-0, 2-2) = <strong>(2, 3, 0)</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Allocation(P1) = (2+1, 0+0, 0+2) = <strong>(3, 0, 2)</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Need(P1) = (1-1, 2-0, 2-2) = <strong>(0, 2, 0)</strong><br>(d) Run the safety check on this new state, Work = (2,3,0):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;P1 Need (0,2,0) ≤ (2,3,0) → YES. Work = (2+3, 3+0, 0+2) = (5, 3, 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;P3 Need (0,1,1) ≤ (5,3,2) → YES. Work = (7, 4, 3)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;P4 Need (4,3,1) ≤ (7,4,3) → YES. Work = (7, 4, 5)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;P0 Need (7,4,3) ≤ (7,4,5) → YES. Work = (7, 5, 5)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;P2 Need (6,0,0) ≤ (7,5,5) → YES. Work = (10, 5, 7)<br><strong>The new state is safe, so the request is GRANTED.</strong> Safe sequence &lt;P1, P3, P4, P0, P2&gt;.<br><br><em>Compare:</em> if instead P0 had requested (0, 2, 0), Available would become (3, 1, 2) and Need(P0) would become (7, 2, 3). Checking: P1 needs (1,2,2) — 2 &gt; 1, no. P2 needs (6,0,0) — 6 &gt; 3, no. P3 needs (0,1,1) — yes, fits. That is how you continue the check for any variation the examiner gives you.",
      "table": {
        "headers": ["", "Prevention", "Avoidance", "Detection & Recovery"],
        "rows": [
          ["Basic idea", "Break one Coffman condition by design", "Grant a request only if the state stays safe", "Let it happen, then find and fix it"],
          ["Needs advance info?", "No", "Yes — the maximum need of every process", "No"],
          ["When does it act?", "At design time", "Before every resource request", "Periodically, after the fact"],
          ["Resource use", "Low (over-restrictive)", "Medium", "High (nothing is restricted)"],
          ["Overhead", "Low at runtime", "High — safety check on every request", "Cost of detection + rollback"],
          ["Example method", "Ordered resource numbering", "Banker's Algorithm", "Wait-for graph cycle detection"]
        ]
      },
      "mistake": "Two errors every year. <strong>(1)</strong> Writing that an unsafe state <em>is</em> a deadlock — it is not; unsafe only means deadlock has become <em>possible</em>. <strong>(2)</strong> Forgetting to compute <code>Need = Max - Allocation</code> and comparing <code>Max</code> against <code>Available</code> instead. Always write the Need table first — it is usually worth marks by itself.",
      "examAnswer": "Deadlocks may be handled by prevention, avoidance, or detection and recovery. Prevention operates at design time by ensuring that at least one of the four necessary conditions can never hold, for example by requiring a process to request all its resources at once, by permitting preemption of held resources, or by imposing a total ordering on resource types and requiring requests in increasing order. Avoidance is less restrictive: it permits the four conditions but requires each process to declare its maximum resource requirement in advance, and it grants a request only if the resulting state remains safe, that is, only if a sequence exists in which every process can obtain its remaining need and terminate. The Banker's Algorithm implements avoidance for multiple instances of each resource type using the matrices Allocation, Max and Need, where Need equals Max minus Allocation, together with the Available vector; its safety algorithm repeatedly selects a process whose Need does not exceed the current Work vector, adds that process's Allocation to Work, and continues until all processes are marked finished. Detection, in contrast, permits deadlock to occur and periodically applies a detection algorithm, after which recovery is achieved either by terminating deadlocked processes or by preempting resources from a selected victim.",
      "remember": "Prevention = never allowed. Avoidance = allowed but checked first (Banker's). Detection = allowed, fixed later. And always: <strong>Need = Max - Allocation</strong>.",
      "why": "Banker's Algorithm is the single most reliable long-answer question in this unit — it is a fixed procedure, so if you practise the steps once you can score full marks on any set of numbers.",
      "practice": "Using the same table above, suppose P4 requests (3, 3, 0). Check the two conditions, then run the safety algorithm on the pretend state and decide whether the request can be granted."
    }
